\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A finite cycle determinant and its circle scaling}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the unit-weight cycle #{C_n}, #{n\ge3}, let #{c>0}
and define #{\omega>0} by #{2\cosh\omega=2+c}. Then
##{\det(cI+L_{C_n})=2\cosh(n\omega)-2
 =4\sinh^2(n\omega /2).}
This uses the nearest-neighbor Laplacian
#{L_{C_n}=2I-T_n-T_n^{-1}}, with every undirected edge counted
once. The condition #{n\ge3} avoids replacing the two-vertex
cycle by a different parallel-edge convention.}

\proof{
\p{Complex Fourier vectors diagonalize #{T_n}; write
#{\zeta_k=e^{2\pi ik/n}} and #{z=e^\omega>1}. The eigenvalues
of #{cI+L_{C_n}} are
##{c+2-\zeta_k-\zeta_k^{-1}
 =z^{-1}(z-\zeta_k)(z-\zeta_k^{-1}).}
Since inversion permutes the #{n}-th roots of unity, their
product is
##{z^{-n}(z^n-1)^2=z^n+z^{-n}-2.}
This equals the real determinant: complexification does not
change the determinant of the original real matrix.}
}

\p{Now fix #{\mu>0}, set #{c=\mu /n^2}, and write
#{\omega_n} for the corresponding solution. Since
#{2\cosh x-2=x^2+O(x^4)} at zero, #{\omega_n\to0} and
#{n\omega_n\to\sqrt\mu}. Therefore
##{\det(\mu n^{-2}I+L_{C_n})
 \longrightarrow4\sinh^2(\sqrt{\mu}/2).}
For the [circle mesh](fdip-004H), with
#{L_n=-n^2L_{C_n}} and #{Q_n=\mu I-L_n}, this is exactly
##{\frac{\det Q_n}{n^{2n}}
 \longrightarrow4\sinh^2(\sqrt{\mu}/2),}
or, in the induced-action notation,
##{\frac12\log\det Q_n-n\log n
 \longrightarrow\log\left(2\sinh(\sqrt{\mu}/2)\right).}
The determinant here is that of the operator #{Q_n}, equivalently
its matrix in an orthonormal basis for the supplied normalized
pairing. In raw vertex coordinates the action-form matrix is
#{Q_n/n}; its determinant carries the additional factor #{n^{-n}}.
Thus a reference measure convention still matters when passing
from this operator calculation to a Gaussian integral.}

\p{This finite-product limit complements the [fixed-mode
covariance limit](fdip-004J) and the [exact cycle covariance](fdip-0057).
It does not define an infinite product without a
normalization, prove convergence of full-field measures, or
identify the limit with a continuum determinant under an unstated
regularization convention. The positive mass is fixed throughout.}
